    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Wait, what do you mean by \"goblin in the garden\"? I'm just trying to figure out if I need to change my soil or something. Is it definitely just nitrogen or something else? How do I check the pH to make sure they can actually absorb the nutrients?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"goblin in the garden\" phrase is just a colloquialism, a way of saying that a nutrient deficiency can be a sneaky problem, like a mischievous goblin lurking in the soil. But, I digress.\n\nTo determine if your soil is the culprit, you'll want to check the pH level. Tomatoes prefer a slightly acidic to neutral soil pH, ranging from 6.0 to 7.0. If your soil is too alkaline, it can lead to nutrient deficiencies, including nitrogen. However, if your soil is too acidic, it can also be a problem.\n\nTo check the pH, you can use a soil testing kit or send a sample to a lab for analysis. If you're feeling adventurous, you can also try the \"vinegar test\": mix 1 tablespoon of vinegar with 1 cup of water and apply it to a small area of the soil. If it fizzes, your soil is likely too alkaline. If it doesn't fizz, it's probably just right.\n\nAs for nitrogen, it's not the only culprit, but it's a common one. You can also check for iron deficiencies, which can cause yellowing leaves, especially on the lower parts of",
      "source": "target",
